a918f0dc-2265-4826-a995-71b1c6c3d59d	kha & krap	📝 ค่ะ & ครับ	40	grammar	<p>\r\n  In Thai, it’s common to add particles at the end of sentences to express tone\r\n  and convey how something is meant. There are many different particles, which\r\n  can express things like politeness, friendliness, excitement, anger, or\r\n  impoliteness.\r\n</p>\r\n\n<p>\n  As a general rule, <a href="tts:ค่ะ">ค่ะ</a> or\n  <a href="tts:ครับ">ครับ</a> can be added to the end of a statement to make\n  it sound more polite and respectful.\n</p>\n<p>\r\n  Two of the most common are <a href="tts:ค่ะ"><strong>ค่ะ</strong></a> (kha)\r\n  and <a href="tts:ครับ"><strong>ครับ</strong></a> (khrap), which are used at\r\n  the end of statements to make them sound more polite and natural.\r\n  Traditionally, <a href="tts:ค่ะ">ค่ะ</a> is associated with female speakers\r\n  and <a href="tts:ครับ">ครับ</a> with male speakers, though usage can vary\r\n  with personal preference and gender expression.\r\n</p>	f	f	f
