55d6be3b-5e29-40da-a009-e27fbd93d47b	basic word order	📝 Basic word order: SVO	41	grammar	<h1>Basic word order: Subject + Verb + Object</h1>\n\n<p>\n  Thai usually follows a simple <strong>Subject + Verb + Object (SVO)</strong>\n  word order, similar to English.\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  For example:\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  <a href="tts:ฉันชอบแมว">ฉันชอบแมว</a><br>\n  <em>chǎn chɔ̂ɔp mɛɛw</em><br>\n  “I like cats.”\n</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Subject:</strong> <a href="tts:ฉัน">ฉัน</a> = I</li>\n  <li><strong>Verb:</strong> <a href="tts:ชอบ">ชอบ</a> = like</li>\n  <li><strong>Object:</strong> <a href="tts:แมว">แมว</a> = cat / cats</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>\n  So the basic pattern is:\n  <strong>Subject + Verb + Object</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  <a href="tts:ฉัน">ฉัน</a> + <a href="tts:ชอบ">ชอบ</a> + <a href="tts:แมว">แมว</a>\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  Thai nouns do not normally change form to show plural, so\n  <a href="tts:แมว">แมว</a> can mean either “cat” or “cats,” depending on context.\n</p>	f	f	f
8c2887d8-c367-4722-901c-da1322549fb3	no to be before adjectives	📝 No “to be” before adjectives	10	grammar	<h1>No “to be” before adjectives</h1>\n\n<p>\n  In Thai, you generally don't need a verb meaning “to be” before an adjective.\n  The adjective can come directly after the subject.\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  For example:\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  <a href="tts:เขาสวย">เขาสวย</a><br>\n  <em>khǎo sǔai</em><br>\n  “She is beautiful.”\n</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Subject:</strong> <a href="tts:เขา">เขา</a> = he / she / they</li>\n  <li><strong>Adjective:</strong> <a href="tts:สวย">สวย</a> = beautiful</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>\n  There is no separate word for “is” in this sentence. The Thai sentence is\n  essentially just:\n  <strong>Subject + Adjective</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  <a href="tts:เขา">เขา</a> + <a href="tts:สวย">สวย</a>\n</p>\n\n<p>\n  This same pattern works with many common adjectives:\n</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><a href="tts:ฉันเหนื่อย">ฉันเหนื่อย</a> — “I am tired.”</li>\n  <li><a href="tts:อากาศร้อน">อากาศร้อน</a> — “The weather is hot.”</li>\n  <li><a href="tts:อาหารอร่อย">อาหารอร่อย</a> — “The food is delicious.”</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>\n  So when you want to say that something <em>is</em> a certain way, don't automatically\n  look for a Thai equivalent of “is.” With adjectives, simply put the adjective\n  after the subject.\n</p>	f	f	f
0c0ae601-b962-43b9-b0fb-4a04a6b2cc98	Car Rental	Car Rental	74	\N	\N	f	f	f
